CBS News confirms the Department of  Homeland Security will bring  unprecedented DNA testing at the border  with Mexico as soon as next week now the  Department says the program will target  human traffickers who are smuggling and  exploiting children it also hopes to  slow illegal crossings that have  overwhelmed border agents Matea via al  is along the border in Hill delgo Texas  Matea good morning  well good morning DHS officials tell us  they are launching this program because  since October they have found about a  thousand fake families trying to cross  the border illegally simply put that's  where an adult is paired with a child  who they are trying to pass off as their  own  now this proposed rapid DNA test will  involve a cheek swab then in about two  hours results will show whether an adult  and a child are actually related now a  private contractor will conduct the  pilot program at at least two locations  right now a record number of families  mostly from Central America have been  crossing the southern border this year  and I recently spoke with some agents  who say this is a very frustrating and  sad part of their job most of the  families that come through here with  children are genuinely looking for a  better life but they have actually seen  cases where children are being used as  pawns because smugglers are telling them  right now it is easier to cross as a  family I've actually talked with one of  these agents who say that they  referenced a case where a young boy was  smuggled several times apprehended  several times over the last few months  come in with different men that were  actually not his family the ACLU is also  weighed in on this they say right now  the DNA testing will intimidate and  deter asylum seekers and while the DHS  has the samples and all the data will be  destroyed after tests are run the ACLU  warns the fact that it is even building  out this surveillance and infrastructure  should trouble us all now a little side  note about this technology it is very  similar to what law enforcement uses out  in California after wildfires to try and  connect families with potential victims  Gail losing some interesting questions  there thank you very much Maria
 

